<p>Six additional wasm-vips files resolved two previously missing comparisons on the second 1024 × 768 photo. The integrated DC comparison uses those frozen peer estimates; the table has {adequate.length} adequately matched pairs and {unresolvedQuality} unresolved pairs. The sampler refines the same nondominated curve used for matching, with the unchanged 0.25-score bracket limit. That peer supplement preserves the original reports and adds no PureJsImage encodes or speed measurements. <a href="/jpeg-xl/evidence/FRONTIER-SAMPLING.md" download>Sampling correction and scope</a>, <a href="/jpeg-xl/evidence/quality-photo-frontier-extension.json" download>Peer measurements and matching calculations</a>, and <a href="/jpeg-xl/evidence/quality-photo-extension.json" download>Earlier five-point supplement</a>.</p>
<p>The integrated opaque palette change improves the original 1024 × 1024 graphic. Its qualified 14,617-byte distance-8 file scores 84.390 in SSIMULACRA2 and 4.820 in Butteraugli, versus 25,580 bytes / 80.153 / 7.669 for one pinned jSquash file and 30,921 bytes / 80.459 / 5.574 for one pinned wasm-vips file. Actual candidate files beat all 24 measured wasm-vips graphic files and 23 of 24 jSquash files in size and both quality metrics. The jSquash quality-101 exception remains. These complete-file comparisons add no tight matched target or competitor speed result. Independent decoder checks, the original 123-file matrix and production tests in Node, Chromium, Firefox and WebKit pass. The runtime tables below retain their earlier source. <a href="/jpeg-xl/evidence/FLAT-PALETTE-CONFIRMATION.md" download>Palette results and limits</a>.</p>
